TI  - MOST BIOLOGICAL EFFECTS OF ZINC-DEFICIENCY CORRECTED BY GAMMA-LINOLENIC ACID (18-3-OMEGA-6) BUT NOT BY LINOLEIC-ACID (18-2-OMEGA-6)
T2  - ATHEROSCLEROSIS
AB  - Zn deficiency mimics many of the clinical features of essential fatty acid (EFA) deficiency in rats. Since Zn appears to be needed for the .delta.-6-desaturase step in EFA metabolism, experiments were conducted to determine whether bypassing this step with .gamma.-linolenic acid (18:3.omega.6) would alleviate the biological effects induced by Zn dificient diet. In pair-fed rats over a period of 5 wk the deficient diet impaired growth and changed the relative weights of internal organs. It also induced hypolipidemia but had little effect on the fatty acid composition of tissue lipids. Daily s.c. injection of primrose oil containing 10% 18:3.omega.6 reversed most of the effects of Zn deficiency on tissue weights, growth and plasma lipids. In contrast, injection of safflower oil, which has a similar content of linoleic acid (18:2.omega.6) but is devoid of 18:3.omega.6, had only a partial effect on some tissue weight changes. Neither oil affected the plasma fatty acid pattern, but both of them increased liver triglyceride concentrations. They also elevated the proportion of 18:2.omega.6 in liver, skin and epididymal fat. The latter effects were not observed in the absence of Zn deficiency. Supplementing the diet of EFA-deficient animals with an excess of Zn in their drinking water did not affect the typical tissue fatty-acid pattern of EFA deficiency. Apparently several of the manifestations of Zn deficiency are mediated by a relative state of EFA deficiency attributable, at least in part, to a reduced conversion of 18:2.omega.6 to 18:3.omega.6 resulting in an accumulation of 18:2.omega.6 in tissues. These findings are consistent with a role of Zn as a cofactor in the .DELTA.-6-desaturase enzyme reaction. The suggested role of Zn in essential fatty acid metabolism may be of significance in understanding how essential fatty acids protect against cardiovascular disease in general.
