TI  - Concentration and ratio of essential fatty acids influences the inflammatory response in lipopolysaccharide challenged mice
T2  - PROSTAGLANDINS LEUKOTRIENES AND ESSENTIAL FATTY ACIDS
AB  - The goal of this study was to evaluate the role of both the % of dietary, 18-carbon PUFA (2.5%, 5% and 10%) and the n-6:n-3 ratio (1:1, 10:1 and 20:1) on the acute inflammatory response. Mice were fed diets for 8 weeks and injected intraperitoneally with LPS to induce acute inflammation. After 24 h mice were sacrificed and plasma cytokines measured. Diets significantly affected the erythrocyte PUFA composition and the effect of PUFA ratio was more prominent than of PUFA concentration. The % dietary PUFA affected feed efficiency (p < 0.05) and there was a PUFA x ratio interaction with body fat (p < 0.01). In mice fed high %kcal from PUFA, those given a low n-6:n-3 ratio had more body fat than those fed a high ratio. Of the twelve cytokines measured, eleven were significantly affected by the % PUFA (p < 0.05), whereas five were affected by the ratio (p < 0.05). For seven cytokines, there was a significant PUFA x ratio interaction according to a two way ANOVA (p < 0.05). These data indicate that dietary polyunsaturated fatty acids can affect LPS induced-inflammation. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
