TI  - Association of fatty acid composition in serum phospholipids with metabolic syndrome and arterial stiffness
T2  - NUTRITION METABOLISM AND CARDIOVASCULAR DISEASES
AB  - Background and aim: We examined the association of fatty acid (FA) composition in serum phospholipids with the features of metabolic syndrome (MetS) and arterial stiffness.
   Methods: Korean men (n=593, 30-79yrs) were categorized based on the number of MetS risk factors (RFs) and measured for the markers of MetS, serum phospholipid FA composition and brachial-ankle pulse wave velocity (baPWV), an index for the severity of arterial stiffness.
   Results: Insulin resistance (HOMA-IR), baPWV, LDL size, and adiponectin were significantly altered corresponding to the number of MetS RFs. The proportions of total monounsaturated FA, palmitoleic acid (16:1), oleic acid (18:1 omega-9) and dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA, 20:3 omega-6) in serum phospholipids, and DGLA/linoleic acid (LA) (20:3 omega-6/18:2 omega-6), deta9-desaturase activity (D9D-16: 16:1/16:0 and D9D-18: 18:1 omega-9/18:0) significantly increased corresponding to the number of MetS RFs, but D5D (20:4 omega u-6/20:3 omega-6) decreased. baPWV positively correlated with HOMA-IR, palmitic acid (16:0), oleic acid, D6D (18:3 omega-6/18:2 omega-6), DGLA/LA and D9D-18, and negatively with adiponectin, LDL size, LA, docosahexaenoic acid (DHA, 22:6 omega-3) and D5D. Multiple stepwise regression models revealed that baPWV was significantly influenced by systolic blood pressure, age, body weight, triglyceride and LA in serum phospholipids (R-2=0.378). Interestingly, baPWV (1419 + 1 cm/s) and MetS (22%) were highest in individuals with lower proportion of LA (<12.361%) and higher proportion of DGLA (>= 1.412%) in serum phospholipid FAs.
   Conclusion: The features of MetS significantly related to serum phosopholipid FA composition. Particularly, arterial stiffness was associated with LA additively together with DLGA. It may suggest a potential benefit of sufficient amounts of LA in serum or in diet can reduce cardiovascular risk. (C) 2011 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
