TI  - Nutritional aspects of fish
T2  - SEAFOOD FROM PRODUCER TO CONSUMER, INTEGRATED APPROACH TO QUALITY
CP  - International Seafood Conference on the Occasion of the 25th Anniversary of the WEFTA
AB  - Epidemiological studies indicate that fish eating populations have a lower risk for coronary heart disease (CHD), hypertension, and cancer. Animal experiments, clinical studies and intervention studies have shown that taking fish oils (rich in eicosapentaenoic acid [PA] and docosahexaenoic acid [DHA]) or increasing the amount of fish in the diet decreased the death rate in persons who already had one episode of myocardial infarction suggesting the protective role of fish or fish oils in the secondary prevention of CHD. In animal experiments fish oil decreased the size and number of tumors. Fish oils have hypolipidemic (lower triglycerides), hypotensive, antithrombotic and antiinflammatory properties through their effects on prostaglandin metabolism, membrane function and cell to cell communication. DHA is essential for normal cerebral and visual function for the premature and possibly for the full term. Infant formula should contain DHA. Similarly, maternal nutrition during pregnancy and lactation should include at least 2-3 fish meals a week.
   Although in recent studies the emphasis has been on the beneficial effects of omega-3 fatty acids, fish is a good source of protein, low in calories, low in saturated fat and cholesterol, but high in omega-3 fatty acids, and is a good source of iron, zinc, copper, fluoride, iodine, calcium, magnesium, potassium, selenium, and vitamins B12, riboflavin and niacin. Fish is a perfect food when free of contaminants and should be eaten freely for general health and prevention of chronic diseases.
