TI  - RADIATION STUDIES OF ACHOLEPLASMA-LAIDLAWII - THE ROLE OF MEMBRANE-COMPOSITION
T2  - INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF RADIATION BIOLOGY
AB  - A. laidlawii, a mycoplasma, is unable to synthesize unsaturated fatty acids but it will incorporate them into its plasma membrane if they are supplied exogeneously. The fatty acid composition of the cell membrane can be defined by growing the organism in media containing speciifc fatty acids. One obtained cells with predominantly one type of unsaturated fatty acid (either oleic, linoleic or linolenic acid) or cells with only saturated fatty acid in the cell membrane. The cells were irradiated with 7 MeV electrons and the effect of membrane fatty acid composition on cell survival was examined. At 200 Gy[Gray]min and 0.5.degree. C (melting ice) there was little difference in the radiation sensitivities of the cells grown in unsaturated fatty acids in aerated or anoxic radiation conditions. The cells containing saturated fatty acids irradiated in anoxic conditions were markedly more sensitive than the cells containing unsaturated fatty acids. At 200 Gy/mn and 37.degree. C the 2 types of cells were of similar sensitivity in aerated and anoxic radiation conditions. At 5 Gy/min at 0.5.degree. C the cells containing linolenic acid (18:3) were less sensitive than those containing solely saturated fatty acids. At 5 Gy/min at 37.degree. C there was no difference in sensitivity between these 2 types of cells. The results strongly argue against the involvement of lipid peroxidation as a molecular change leading to cell death.
