TI  - FATTY-ACID PATTERN OF RED-BLOOD-CELL MEMBRANES AND RISK OF ISCHEMIC BRAIN INFARCTION - A CASE-CONTROL STUDY
T2  - STROKE
AB  - The fatty acid composition of red blood cell membranes (which reflects dietary fat intake) was studied in 28 male patients with recent (< 3 days) ischemic stroke and 56 matched controls. Fifteen fatty acids were measured by means of chromatographic analysis. Percentages of linoleic, 22:5, and 22:6 acids were significntly lower in red blood cell membranes of stroke patients than in those of matched controls. The results suggest that a low unsaturated fatty acid diet could be an independent risk  factor for ischemic brain infarction.
