TI  - PLATELET-FUNCTION, THROMBOXANE FORMATION AND BLOOD-PRESSURE CONTROL DURING SUPPLEMENTATION OF THE WESTERN DIET WITH COD LIVER OIL
T2  - CIRCULATION
AB  - Epidemiologic and experimental data suggest an antiatherothrombotic potential of .omega.-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids. The Western diet, which supplies predominantly .omega.-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids, was supplemented with 40 ml/day of cod liver oil, which provides .apprx. 10 g of .omega.-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids daily, for 25 days in 8 volunteers. The .omega.-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids were incorporated in platelet and erythrocyte membrane phospholipids at the expense of .omega.-6 polunsaturated fatty acids. Bleeding time increased (P < 0.01) and platelet count (P < 0.05), platelet aggregation upon ADP and collagen (P < 0.01-0.05) and associated thromboxane B2 formation (P < 0.01) decreased. Blood pressure (P < 0.05) and blood pressure response to norepinephrine (P < 0.01) and angiotensin II (NS) [not significant]) fell, without major changes in plasma catecholamines, renin, urinary aldosterone, kallikrein, prostaglandins E2 and F2.alpha. and red cell cation fluxes. Biochemical and functional changes were reversed 4 wk after cod liver oil was discontinued. Formation of prostaglandins derived from eicosapentaenoic acid and interference of eicosapentaenoic acid with formation and action of prostaglandins derived from arachidonic acid were evident in vitro. Whatever the mechanism, this moderate supplement of .omega.-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids markedly changed membrane phospholipids, which was associated with a shift toward less reactive platelets and a blunted circulatory response to pressure hormones. [The protective potential of dietary eicosapentaenoic acid against cardiovascular disease is discussed.].
